\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

The event splits the participants into three teams
(professors/tenure-track, lecturers, PhD students) who relay-build a
web game: one member at the keyboard at a time, roughly ten-minute
turns, a shared token budget.  Three properties drive the whole
design.

\begin{description}
\item[Fresh sessions]  Each turn starts a new [[claude]] session with
  no chat history.  The \emph{files} the team leaves behind---most
  importantly their [[CLAUDE.md]]---are the only memory that carries
  over.  That is the pedagogical point: the way to help your
  teammates is harness engineering, not longer chats.
\item[Shared team directory]  Everyone on a team works on the same
  project, so all of a team's sessions must run in one directory.
\item[Persistence for evaluation]  After the event we evaluate both
  the artefacts and \emph{how} they were produced, so the project
  files and Claude's session transcripts must survive the container.
\end{description}

The facilitator's workflow is three [[make]] targets on their own
laptop, run any day before the event:
\begin{verbatim}
claude setup-token   # once: prints the sk-ant-... token
TEAMS="the-np-hard-partiers"
TEAMS="${TEAMS} big-o-no-you-didnt"
TEAMS="${TEAMS} the-halting-problems"
TEAMS="${TEAMS} lambda-lambda-lambda"
TEAMS="${TEAMS} p=np-after-two-beers"
export TEAMS
make prepare    # seed .data/: homes + Claude config
make build      # build the Docker image
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-... make run  # start
\end{verbatim}
followed by writing the laptop's IP address on the board.  The first
line is a one-off: [[claude setup-token]] prints a long-lived token on
the facilitator's own machine, which is then pasted into the
[[CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=]] of the run line.  The teams---their number
and names---are whatever the space-separated [[TEAMS]] variable lists,
honoured by both [[make prepare]] and [[make run]] (so the [[export]]
above, or [[TEAMS="..."]] on each command, is all it takes to add or
rename teams; omit it for the default three).  The post-game hacker
space is a second, independent container, brought up later with one
more command:
\begin{verbatim}
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-... make hackspace
\end{verbatim}
\Cref{sec:buildrun} documents each target, the credential, [[TEAMS]]
and the hacker space; \cref{sec:prepare} covers what [[make prepare]]
seeds.  Participants then simply run
\begin{center}
  [[ssh -p 2222 groupa@<ip-address>]]
\end{center}
and find themselves in their team's Claude session.

\section{Overview}\label{sec:overview}

\Cref{fig:overview} shows the moving pieces.  The container runs only
an SSH daemon.  Each team has a Unix account whose \emph{login shell}
is our launcher script: logging in \emph{is} starting Claude, and
when Claude exits the connection closes.  This is what makes
\enquote{fresh session per connect} free---there is no terminal
multiplexer to reattach to and no session to resume by accident.

