The persistence design hinges on one observation: Claude Code stores
each session's transcript as a [[.jsonl]] file under
[[~/.claude/projects/]], keyed by the working directory.  If we make
each team
account's \emph{home directory} also be its \emph{working directory},
then the transcripts land inside the very directory we already
persist for the project files.  One volume mount
([[-v <hostdir>:/data]]) captures everything the post-event
evaluation needs:

\begin{verbatim}
.data/
|-- ssh/                                 persisted SSH host keys
`-- groups/{groupa,groupb,groupc}/
    |-- CLAUDE.md, RULES.md, ...         seeds + project files
    `-- .claude/projects/<hash>/*.jsonl  session transcripts
\end{verbatim}

Because the home directories persist on the host, all seeding---the
relay rules, a starter [[CLAUDE.md]] and Claude's configuration---can
happen \emph{outside} the container, any time before the event
(\cref{sec:prepare}).  The
container itself stays stateless: its entry point only recreates the
team accounts, fixes ownership, and starts the SSH daemon
(\cref{sec:entrypoint}).  We can rebuild or restart it freely without
losing anything.

The remaining pieces are the image itself (\cref{sec:dockerfile}),
the launcher (\cref{sec:launcher}), the SSH configuration
(\cref{sec:sshd}), the seed content (\cref{sec:seeds}), the
facilitator's build-and-run instructions (\cref{sec:buildrun}), and
finally harvesting the results once the event is over
(\cref{sec:harvest}).

\section{The image}\label{sec:dockerfile}

The image is deliberately boring: Node, an SSH server, Claude Code,
and our three runtime scripts.

<<[[Dockerfile]]>>=
FROM node:22-bookworm-slim

<<install system packages>>
<<install Claude Code>>
<<install the statusline tools>>
<<allow empty passwords in PAM>>
<<silence the login banner>>
<<event environment defaults>>
<<install the runtime files>>

EXPOSE 22
EXPOSE 8000-8099
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh"]
@

We start from a Node image rather than a plain Debian one because
Claude Code is an npm package, and the event task is an HTML5/vanilla
JavaScript game, so Node doubles as the participants' runtime should
they want one.  The [[slim]] variant keeps the image small; we add
back exactly the tools an agent (or a curious participant) plausibly
needs: git, a fetcher, Python, a fast grep, a pager and an editor.
[[procps]] gives the facilitator [[ps]] for a quick look at who is
running what.

<<install system packages>>=
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      openssh-server git ca-certificates curl python3 \
      ripgrep less nano procps \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
@

We pin the Claude Code version through a build argument so that the
image we rehearse with is reproducibly the image we run at the event;
[[latest]] is only the default for casual builds.

<<install Claude Code>>=
ARG CLAUDE_VERSION=latest
RUN npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code@${CLAUDE_VERSION}
@

Each session shows a status line (\cref{sec:seeds}): the model and git
state, the rolling token usage, and a usage breakdown from
[[ccusage]].  The renderer is [[ccstatusline]] and the usage figures
come from [[ccusage]]; the status line is redrawn constantly, so how it
is supplied matters.  We bake both tools into the image rather than
fetch them at render time.  A pre-installed binary on [[PATH]] renders
instantly from the first connection and needs no network during the
event---the same reasoning that makes us pin [[CLAUDE_VERSION]] and
persist the SSH host keys (\cref{sec:entrypoint}): an academic's
ten-minute turn should not be spent watching a download, and the venue
network is not something we want a working status line to depend on.
The versions are pinned through build arguments for the same
reproducibility reason as Claude Code itself.

<<install the statusline tools>>=
ARG CCSTATUSLINE_VERSION=latest
ARG CCUSAGE_VERSION=latest
RUN npm install -g ccstatusline@${CCSTATUSLINE_VERSION} \
      ccusage@${CCUSAGE_VERSION}
@

The team accounts have \emph{empty} passwords
(\cref{sec:sshd})---participants should type one command and be in.
On Debian, [[sshd]]'s [[PermitEmptyPasswords]] is necessary but not
sufficient: with [[UsePAM yes]] the actual password check is done by
[[pam_unix]], which rejects empty passwords unless its [[nullok]]
option is set.  We edit [[common-auth]] idempotently---only lines
that mention [[pam_unix.so]] and do not already say [[nullok]] are
touched---so rebuilding on a base image that changes its PAM defaults
cannot double-append the option.

<<allow empty passwords in PAM>>=
RUN sed -ri '/pam_unix\.so/ {/nullok/! s/$/ nullok/}' \
      /etc/pam.d/common-auth
@

Logging in should land the participant in Claude and nothing else.
Setting [[PrintMotd no]] in the SSH configuration (\cref{sec:sshd})
is not enough on Debian: [[pam_motd]] prints the distribution's
\enquote{The programs included with\dots} banner on every login
regardless of what [[sshd]] thinks, so we drop it from the PAM stack
altogether.

<<silence the login banner>>=
RUN sed -i '/pam_motd/d' /etc/pam.d/sshd
@

Four knobs are environment variables with sensible defaults, all
overridable at [[docker run]] time.  [[TEAMS]] names the accounts to
create.  [[ANTHROPIC_MODEL]] starts everyone on [[claude-sonnet-4-6]],
capable enough for the game while leaving Opus as a conscious upgrade;
teams can switch with [[/model]], and weighing that against the shared
token budget is part of the game.  [[CLAUDE_FLAGS]] defaults to
[[--permission-mode auto]], which lets Claude handle permissions
automatically so prompts do not eat the participants' ten-minute
turns.  We deliberately avoid [[--dangerously-skip-permissions]]: the
sessions run with real credentials in a shared container, so an
unattended, no-questions bypass is not a trade we want.  [[WEB_PORTS]]
is the range a session may serve test builds on (\cref{sec:webtest}); a
default lives here so the variable is always defined, but the value
that matters is the matching range the [[Makefile]] actually publishes,
which is why the hacker space can override it to a non-clashing range.

<<event environment defaults>>=
ENV TEAMS="groupa groupb groupc"
ENV ANTHROPIC_MODEL="claude-sonnet-4-6"
ENV CLAUDE_FLAGS="--permission-mode auto"
ENV WEB_PORTS="8000-8099"
@

Finally the runtime files: the entry point, the launcher and the
harvest script (\cref{sec:harvest}) go into the path, the SSH
configuration replaces Debian's default outright (so we reason about
one file, not a diff against the distribution's), and [[/data]] exists
as the mount point.

<<install the runtime files>>=
COPY --chmod=755 entrypoint.sh group-session team-report \
  /usr/local/bin/
COPY sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config
RUN mkdir -p /data/groups
@

\section{Runtime: the entry point}\label{sec:entrypoint}

The entry point is the only thing that runs as root.  It validates
the credential, makes the runtime environment reachable from SSH
sessions, arranges stable host keys, recreates the team accounts
against the mounted volume, pre-approves the API key in each team's
Claude configuration, and finally becomes the SSH daemon.

<<[[entrypoint.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

<<require a credential>>
<<write the event environment file>>
<<persist the SSH host keys>>
<<create the team accounts>>
<<approve the API key for each team>>
<<start the SSH daemon>>
@

\subsection{Requiring a credential}

There are two ways to pay for the event: a Claude subscription (a
long-lived OAuth token minted once with [[claude setup-token]] on the
facilitator's machine) or API credits.  Claude Code accepts either
through an environment variable, so the container takes whichever is
present.  Failing fast here, with a message that names both options,
beats the alternative---a container that starts fine and then greets
every participant with an authentication error.

<<require a credential>>=
if [ -z "${CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN:-}" ] \
   && [ -z "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ]
then
  echo "error: no credential; run the container with either" >&2
  echo "  -e CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN  (claude setup-token)" >&2
  echo "  -e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY        (API credits)" >&2
  exit 1
fi
@

\subsection{The event environment file}\label{sec:eventenv}

This is the one genuinely non-obvious mechanic in the container.
Variables passed with [[docker run -e]] reach the entry point, but
\emph{not} the shells that [[sshd]] spawns: an SSH login gets a fresh
environment built from PAM and the login shell, not from PID~1's.  So
the entry point serialises the runtime configuration to a file, and
the launcher (\cref{sec:launcher}) sources that file before starting
Claude.  Alongside the credential and model, the list carries
[[HOST_IP]]---the address participants reached the laptop on---so that a
session can hand a player the complete URL of a test server it spins up
for them (\cref{sec:webtest}).  It is the one value Claude cannot work
out for itself: [[sshd]] inside the container sees the connection
arriving on Docker's internal network, not the laptop's address on the
room's LAN.

<<write the event environment file>>=
: > /etc/event.env
for var in CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN ANTHROPIC_API_KEY \
           ANTHROPIC_MODEL CLAUDE_FLAGS HOST_IP WEB_PORTS
do
  eval "value=\${$var:-}"
  if [ -n "$value" ]
  then
    printf 'export %s="%s"\n' "$var" "$value" >> /etc/event.env
  fi
done
chmod 644 /etc/event.env
@

The file is world-readable, which means participants can read the
credential.  This is not an oversight: the sessions \emph{run} with
that credential, so anyone could equally ask Claude to print its own
environment.  The protection is not file permissions but the
credential itself---a budget-capped API key or a token the
facilitator revokes after the event.

\subsection{Persisting the SSH host keys}

Docker containers are rebuilt and restarted; SSH clients remember
host keys.  If the keys changed on every rebuild, every participant
would hit the dreaded \enquote{remote host identification has
changed} wall mid-event.  We therefore keep the keys on the volume:
on first start we let [[ssh-keygen -A]] fill in any missing keys and
copy them out to [[/data/ssh]]; on every later start we copy them
back in.  We copy rather than symlink into the volume because
[[sshd]] refuses keys with lax permissions, and permissions on
host-mounted volumes are whatever the host happens to have---copying
lets us reset ownership and mode unconditionally.

<<persist the SSH host keys>>=
mkdir -p /data/ssh
if [ -n "$(ls -A /data/ssh)" ]
then
  cp /data/ssh/ssh_host_* /etc/ssh/
else
  ssh-keygen -A
  cp /etc/ssh/ssh_host_* /data/ssh/
fi
chown root:root /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*
chmod 600 /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key
chmod 644 /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub
@

\subsection{Creating the team accounts}

The accounts live in [[/etc]], which is \emph{not} persisted---so
they are recreated on every start, idempotently, against the
persistent home directories.  Two details matter.

The UIDs are pinned (2001, 2002, \dots, in [[TEAMS]] order) so that
file ownership on the volume is stable across container runs; without
pinning, a future start could allocate UIDs in a different order and
silently swap the teams' file ownership.  The [[chown -R]] then makes
the account the owner of whatever is in its home directory---in
particular the seed files, which were written on the host by whoever
ran [[prepare.sh]] (\cref{sec:prepare}) and therefore arrive with a
host user's UID.

[[useradd --no-create-home]] plus our own [[mkdir -p]] keeps the
account creation working both when the volume is already seeded and
when someone starts an unseeded container to poke around.

<<create the team accounts>>=
uid=2001
for team in $TEAMS
do
  if ! id "$team" >/dev/null 2>&1
  then
    useradd --uid "$uid" --home-dir "/data/groups/$team" \
      --no-create-home --shell /usr/local/bin/group-session "$team"
    passwd -d "$team"
  fi
  mkdir -p "/data/groups/$team"
  chown -R "$team:$team" "/data/groups/$team"
  uid=$((uid + 1))
done
@

[[passwd -d]] removes the password rather than locking the account:
combined with [[PermitEmptyPasswords]] and PAM's [[nullok]]
(\cref{sec:dockerfile,sec:sshd}) this is what lets participants log
in with no password at all.

\subsection{Pre-approving the API key}

When Claude Code finds an [[ANTHROPIC_API_KEY]] in the environment,
it asks---once per account---whether to use it.  It records the
answer under [[customApiKeyResponses]] in [[~/.claude.json]], as the
key's last twenty characters.  The subscription token
([[CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN]]) does not trigger this question, so the
recommended credential path is unaffected; but if the event falls
back to an API key, the first member of every team would burn the
start of their turn on a security question they cannot judge anyway.
The seed configuration cannot answer it, because the key is only
known at [[docker run]] time---so the entry point answers it, here.

The merge is done in Python rather than shell because editing JSON
with [[sed]] is how config files get corrupted, and the image ships
[[python3]] anyway.  The [[chown]] afterwards is needed because this
runs as root after the accounts' homes have already been handed over
to the teams.

<<approve the API key for each team>>=
if [ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ]
then
  for team in $TEAMS
  do
    config="/data/groups/$team/.claude.json"
    python3 - "$config" "$ANTHROPIC_API_KEY" <<'EOF'
<<record the key as approved>>
EOF
    chown "$team:$team" "$config"
  done
fi
@

The script tolerates a missing configuration file (an unseeded
volume) by starting from an empty one; everything else it leaves
untouched.

<<record the key as approved>>=
import json
import sys

path, key = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
try:
    with open(path) as f:
        config = json.load(f)
except FileNotFoundError:
    config = {}
approved = config.setdefault(
    "customApiKeyResponses", {}).setdefault("approved", [])
if key[-20:] not in approved:
    approved.append(key[-20:])
with open(path, "w") as f:
    json.dump(config, f, indent=2)
@

\subsection{Starting the SSH daemon}

Debian's [[sshd]] refuses to start without its privilege-separation
directory, and [[/run]] is a fresh tmpfs in every container---a
classic sshd-in-Docker trap, so we create it explicitly.  We [[exec]]
so that [[sshd]] becomes PID~1 and receives the signals from
[[docker stop]]; [[-D]] keeps it in the foreground (a daemonising
PID~1 would end the container), and [[-e]] sends the log to stderr
where [[docker logs]] picks it up.

<<start the SSH daemon>>=
mkdir -p /run/sshd
exec /usr/sbin/sshd -D -e
@

\section{Runtime: the launcher}\label{sec:launcher}

The launcher is each team account's \emph{login shell}.  That single
design choice does most of the work in this project: every SSH login
runs it, there is no other shell to fall back into, and when Claude
exits the connection closes---so \enquote{fresh session per connect}
needs no further machinery.  Note what this also means: two teammates
connecting concurrently get two independent Claude sessions in the
same directory.  That is intended; coordinating is the team's
problem, and a productive part of the game.

It sources the event environment file because, as explained in
\cref{sec:entrypoint}, an SSH session does not inherit the
container's [[docker run -e]] environment.  It changes to [[$HOME]]
explicitly because the working directory is the contract---Claude
must run in the team directory for the transcripts to land there.
Finally it [[exec]]s Claude rather than running it as a child: the
launcher has nothing left to do, and leaving a useless parent shell
around would only add a process between [[sshd]] and Claude.

<<[[group-session]]>>=
#!/bin/sh
. /etc/event.env
cd "$HOME"
exec claude $CLAUDE_FLAGS
@

[[$CLAUDE_FLAGS]] is deliberately unquoted: it is a list of flags,
not a single argument, and the word splitting is the point.

There is deliberately no first-session special case: with the
specification withheld to paper (\cref{sec:seeds}), there is nothing
for the launcher to reveal, so every connection runs the same three
lines.

\section{SSH configuration}\label{sec:sshd}

The configuration replaces Debian's default file wholesale, so this
section is the complete truth about how the daemon behaves.

<<[[sshd_config]]>>=
PermitRootLogin no
PasswordAuthentication yes
PermitEmptyPasswords yes
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no
UsePAM yes
X11Forwarding no
AllowTcpForwarding no
ClientAliveInterval 60
PrintMotd no
PrintLastLog no
AcceptEnv LANG LC_*
@

Empty passwords are a deliberate trade-off, not an accident: the
container runs for under an hour, on a laptop, on the event's local
network, and the accounts can do nothing but run Claude in a sandbox.
The UX win---academics type one command and are in---outweighs the
risk.  If the event network turns out to be hostile, the fallback is
two lines in the entry point: replace [[passwd -d]] with
[[echo "$team:summer" | chpasswd]] and drop
[[PermitEmptyPasswords]].

The rest of the file removes capabilities we do not want to hand
out: no root login, no X11, no TCP forwarding (the container should
not become anyone's jump host).  [[KbdInteractiveAuthentication no]]
forces the plain password path, which is the one we configured PAM's
[[nullok]] for.  [[ClientAliveInterval]] cleans up sessions whose
laptops wandered off to another room.  Omitting Debian's usual
[[Subsystem sftp]] line disables SFTP/SCP entirely---file transfer
in or out goes through the facilitator and the host volume, not
through participants' clients.

\section{Host-side preparation}\label{sec:prepare}

Seeding is the facilitator's job, on the host, any time before the
event---this script never runs in the container.  The split follows
from persistence (\cref{sec:overview}): since the team homes live on
the host volume, anything placed there before the container starts
is simply \emph{there} when the first participant connects.  Keeping
the container free of seeding logic also means restarting it can
never clobber a team's work.

<<[[prepare.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

<<determine directories and teams>>

for team in $teams
do
  <<seed the team home directory>>
done
@

The script takes the data directory and the team names as arguments,
with defaults matching the container's ([[TEAMS]] in
\cref{sec:dockerfile}); the seed sources are located relative to the
script itself so it can be run from anywhere.

<<determine directories and teams>>=
scriptdir="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
datadir="${1:-.data}"
if [ $# -gt 0 ]
then
  shift
fi
teams="${*:-groupa groupb groupc}"
@

Seeding is idempotent so the facilitator can re-run it after editing
the templates: a file is only copied when the destination does not
yet exist, so files a team (or an earlier run) already created are
never clobbered.  (GNU [[cp -n]] would say this in one flag, but it
nowadays warns that its no-clobber behaviour is non-portable, so we
spell the check out.)  Ownership is
deliberately \emph{not} handled here---it would require root, and the
container's entry point [[chown]]s the homes at every start anyway.

The loop globs the template twice because some seeds are dotfile
directories.  A plain [[template/*]] never matches names beginning
with a dot, yet the status-line configuration (\cref{sec:seeds}) lives
in [[.claude/]] and [[.config/]]; the second pattern, [[.[!.]*]],
picks those up while excluding [[.]] and [[..]].  When a pattern
matches nothing the shell leaves it unexpanded, so the existence guard
skips the literal non-match rather than trying to copy it.  [[cp -r]]
already recurses, so a whole [[.claude/]] tree copies in one step; the
existence check is per top-level entry, which keeps the coarse
idempotency---a home that already has a [[.claude/]] is left alone
wholesale.

The Claude configuration is the one file that cannot be a plain
copy: Claude Code tracks per-project trust by the project's absolute
path as seen \emph{inside} the container,
[[/data/groups/<team>]], which differs for each team.  We therefore
instantiate it from a template by substituting the placeholder.

<<seed the team home directory>>=
home="$datadir/groups/$team"
mkdir -p "$home"
for file in "$scriptdir/template/"* "$scriptdir/template/".[!.]*
do
  [ -e "$file" ] || continue
  if [ ! -e "$home/$(basename "$file")" ]
  then
    cp -r "$file" "$home/"
  fi
done
if [ ! -f "$home/.claude.json" ]
then
  sed "s|HOMEDIR|/data/groups/$team|" \
    "$scriptdir/claude-defaults/.claude.json" > "$home/.claude.json"
fi
@

\section{Seed content}\label{sec:seeds}

\subsection{Claude configuration}

Without configuration, the first [[claude]] run greets the user with
a few one-time questions: a theme picker and a workspace-trust
dialogue.  Burning the first minutes of the first team member's turn
on questions only the facilitator can meaningfully answer would be a
poor start, so we pre-answer them.  (The API-key approval is the one answer that
cannot live here, since the key is only known at run time; the entry
point handles it, \cref{sec:entrypoint}.)  [[HOMEDIR]] is the
placeholder that [[prepare.sh]] substitutes with the team's
in-container home.

<<[[.claude.json]]>>=
{
  "hasCompletedOnboarding": true,
  "theme": "dark",
  "projects": {
    "HOMEDIR": {
      "hasTrustDialogAccepted": true,
      "hasCompletedProjectOnboarding": true
    }
  }
}
@

\subsection{Status line}

The status line is configured in two files because the work is split
between two tools.  Claude Code only needs to know \emph{which command}
to run for its status line; the layout---what to show and in what
colour---is [[ccstatusline]]'s own concern, kept in [[ccstatusline]]'s
own configuration file.  Separating them this way means the layout can
be reshaped without touching Claude's settings, and Claude's settings
stay small---the status-line entry point and the default model, nothing
more.

Both files seed straight into the team home, which is also the
in-container [[HOME]] (\cref{sec:overview}), so they land where each
tool looks: [[~/.claude/settings.json]] for Claude and
[[~/.config/ccstatusline/settings.json]] for the renderer.  Neither
needs the path substitution that [[.claude.json]] does---nothing in
them depends on the team's directory---so they are plain seed files the
facilitator can edit before the event.

Claude's settings name the renderer directly.  Because both tools are
baked into the image and on [[PATH]] (\cref{sec:dockerfile}), the
command is just [[ccstatusline]]---no [[bunx]] wrapper to resolve a
package at render time, which is what keeps the line instant and
offline.

They also pin the model.  The [[ANTHROPIC_MODEL]] environment variable
(\cref{sec:dockerfile}) is meant to choose it, but an environment
variable is only as good as its propagation: should it fail to reach
Claude---unset on the host, dropped on the way through [[docker run]],
or lost before the launcher sources it---Claude falls back to the
account's own default, which need not be the small model the game is
budgeted for.  Naming the model in the seed makes
[[claude-sonnet-4-6]] the baseline that holds regardless.  The
environment variable still wins when it \emph{does} arrive, so the
hacker space's larger model (\cref{sec:overview}, [[HACK_MODEL]]) keeps
overriding the seed at run time; the seed only decides what happens
when nothing overrides it.

<<[[.claude/settings.json]]>>=
{
  "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  "statusLine": {
    "type": "command",
    "command": "ccstatusline",
    "padding": 0
  }
}
@

The renderer's own configuration lays out three lines.  The first is
the working context---model, remaining context length, and the git
branch and dirty-file count---so a player can see at a glance what they
are working with.  The second is the shared budget the relay runs on:
the rolling session usage, when it resets, and the weekly total.  The
third defers to [[ccusage]] for a fuller usage breakdown; like
[[ccstatusline]] it is on [[PATH]], so the custom command is plainly
[[ccusage statusline]].

