\subsection{Team instructions}

The relay is a game of telephone, and the seed content is shaped by
what we deliberately \emph{withhold}.  The specification never enters
the container: the first player is handed it on paper, and everyone
after knows only what their teammates left behind.  With no
[[SPEC.md]] on the machine---and no copy of it smuggled into
[[CLAUDE.md]]---a fresh session genuinely cannot recover the spec, so
the degradation along the relay is real rather than honour-system.
Giving the teams little is the point; working the rest out is the
game.

What we seed is therefore deliberately thin.  [[CLAUDE.md]], which
Claude Code loads into every session automatically, does just enough
to be a good host: it says a relay game is afoot and points at
[[RULES.md]], so a player can ask Claude how the game works and get a
straight answer without having to know the file is there.  It stops
short of the task itself---no spec, no hints about what to build.  The
one capability it does spell out is how to preview the work in a
browser (\cref{sec:webtest}): the deliverable is a web app, so a player
who wants to \emph{see} what the team has built so far should not have
to reinvent the serving recipe under a ten-minute clock.  This reveals
the medium, not the task---which problem to solve still arrives only on
the first player's printed sheet.

<<[[CLAUDE.md]]>>=
# Team workspace

You are helping a team play a relay game: building a small project
together, one teammate at the keyboard at a time, with a fresh
session every turn.

How the relay works — turns, what carries over between sessions, what
counts as fair play — is written in RULES.md.  Read it when a player
asks how the game works or what is allowed.

When a player wants to see the project running in a browser, you can
serve it over HTTP.  The container publishes the port range in the
$WEB_PORTS environment variable (for example 8000-8099) to the outside;
pick a free port in that range and start a simple static server bound
to 0.0.0.0 on it from the project directory, for example
`python3 -m http.server <port> --bind 0.0.0.0`.  Then tell the player
to open `http://$HOST_IP:<port>/` — the same address they SSH'd to,
with that port.  If the port is already taken, another team is probably
using it, so pick another free one in the range.  If $HOST_IP is empty,
tell the player to reuse the IP address from their ssh command.
@

[[RULES.md]] carries the rules themselves and says outright that the
telephone dynamic is intentional, so nobody mistakes a withheld
specification for a broken setup.

<<[[RULES.md]]>>=
# Rules of the relay

<!-- TODO: confirm turn length, budget and veto rules before the
     event. -->

1. One team member at the keyboard at a time, in turns of roughly
   ten minutes.
2. Only the first player is shown the full specification.  Everyone
   after that knows only what their teammates left behind.
3. Every connection starts a fresh Claude session: no chat history
   survives between turns.  Only the files in this directory do.
4. The team shares one token budget for the whole relay; spending it
   wisely is part of the game.
5. Anything not violating these rules is fair play.
@

The specification stays out of all of this.  It is Ric's
\enquote{Academic Maze Royale} concept: the maze game from his Knowit
dry run, reskinned so the loot is academic currency---citations,
grants, PhD students.  We record its text here, since it lives neither
in the seed content nor in the container; this is the sheet Ric prints
and hands the first player:

\begin{verbatim}
# Tenure Track — The Academic Maze Royale

Build a web-based, two-player maze game.  Players
compete against the clock to gather academic loot:

- Citations
- Research grants
- PhD students (who trail slowly behind the player)

Players are hunted by Reviewer #2 and get a speed
boost from random coffee cups.
\end{verbatim}

We keep it as terse as that slide on purpose: the relay degrades
detail on its own, and pinning down exact scores, timings or a finish
condition would only fight the game and the teams' own design
choices.


\section{Building and running}\label{sec:buildrun}

The [[Makefile]] (committed alongside this document, since it cannot
tangle itself) wraps the workflow:

\begin{description}
\item[ [[make]] ]  tangles all the files above and weaves this PDF;
\item[ [[make prepare]] ]  seeds [[.data/]] on the host
  (\cref{sec:prepare});
\item[ [[make build]] ]  builds the Docker image;
\item[ [[make run]] ]  starts the container, publishing port 2222 and
  the [[8000]]--[[8099]] range (\cref{sec:webtest}) and mounting
  [[.data/]]; the credential, [[TEAMS]] and [[HOST_IP]] are taken from
  the calling environment.
\end{description}

The team accounts default to [[groupa groupb groupc]], but their number
and names are just the space-separated [[TEAMS]] variable, which both
[[make prepare]] and [[make run]] honour:
\begin{verbatim}
export TEAMS="profs lecturers phds"
make prepare
make run
\end{verbatim}
(equivalently [[make prepare TEAMS="..."]] on each line).  The same
list deliberately drives both targets because they touch different
halves of the same accounts: [[make prepare]] creates the home
directories on the host volume, while [[make run]] passes [[TEAMS]]
into the container, whose entry point creates the matching Unix
accounts against those homes (\cref{sec:prepare,sec:dockerfile}).  A
mismatch would leave a team with a home but no account, or an account
pointing at an unseeded home---so keeping it one variable is what keeps
the two halves in step.

Port 2222 rather than 22 because the facilitator's laptop most
likely runs its own SSH daemon on 22; participants connect with
[[ssh -p 2222 groupa@<ip-address>]].  No client software needs
installing: macOS and Linux ship an SSH client, and so does
Windows~10 and later (in PowerShell), so PuTTY or WSL are not
required.

On capacity: an idle Claude session holds roughly 200\,MB of memory,
so even an unrealistic fifty \emph{simultaneous} sessions need about
10\,GB---a modern laptop hosts the event comfortably, and the SSH
daemon's connection-rate defaults are nowhere near a bottleneck at
this scale.

\subsection{Seeing the game in a browser}\label{sec:webtest}

The deliverable is a web app, but participants reach the machine over a
text-only SSH session, so there is no on-screen browser to open the
work in.  They view it from their own laptop instead: when a player
wants to try the game, Claude serves it over HTTP and the player points
a browser at the laptop running the container.  For that to reach
anything, two pieces have to line up.

First, the port has to cross the container boundary.  [[make run]]
publishes the whole [[8000]]--[[8099]] range, so a server Claude starts
on any free port in it is reachable from the room.  A range rather than
a single port because all teams share one container: each running game
needs its own port, and Claude simply picks one that is free.  The
published range and the range the seed offers Claude have to be the
same set of numbers, so rather than hard-code [[8000]]--[[8099]] in two
places we pass it in once as [[WEB_PORTS]] (\cref{sec:eventenv}); the
seed (\cref{sec:seeds}) reads the range from there, tells Claude to
stay inside it, and to bind [[0.0.0.0]] rather than [[localhost]], since
a server bound only to the loopback interface would be invisible from
outside the container.  Threading the range through a variable is what
lets the hacker space (\cref{sec:hackspace}) run a \emph{second}
container on the same laptop: it publishes a different range and passes
that same range as its [[WEB_PORTS]], so the two never fight over a host
port.  Note that this is ordinary Docker port publishing, unrelated to
[[AllowTcpForwarding no]] (\cref{sec:sshd})---that switch disables SSH
\emph{tunnelling}, a different mechanism that we still want off.

Second, the player needs the right address.  Claude knows the port it
chose but cannot discover the laptop's LAN address from inside the
container, so we feed that address in as [[HOST_IP]]
(\cref{sec:eventenv}) and let the session assemble the full
[[http://$HOST_IP:<port>/]] URL.  [[make run]] fills [[HOST_IP]] in
automatically from the host's primary address, overridable when that
guess picks the wrong interface.

\subsection{A second container: the hacker space}\label{sec:hackspace}

The event runs in three stages---lobby (survey and discussion),
blind build (the relay game), and a hacker space where people who
have finished drip in and play freely, possibly on a larger model.
The same image serves the hacker space as a \emph{second container},
with its own name, port, account list, model and data volume.  A
single [[make hackspace]] brings it up: it seeds [[.hackdata/]] and
starts a container named [[hackspace]] on port 2223, defaulting to
four [[hack]] accounts on the larger [[claude-opus-4-8]] model.
Like [[make run]] it reads the credential from the environment, so
pass the token the same way:

\begin{verbatim}
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-... make hackspace
\end{verbatim}

Each default is its own variable---[[HACK_TEAMS]], [[HACK_PORT]],
[[HACK_MODEL]], [[HACK_WEB_PORTS]]---so the room can be reshaped without
editing the [[Makefile]].  [[HACK_WEB_PORTS]] defaults to
[[8100]]--[[8199]], a different range from the game's, so the hacker
space can publish its own test-server ports without clashing with a
game container still running on the same laptop (\cref{sec:webtest}):

\begin{verbatim}
make hackspace HACK_TEAMS="alice bob carol" HACK_PORT=2224
\end{verbatim}

The separate volume is the important part: the game's
[[.data/]] stays pristine for the evaluation, no matter what the
hacker space gets up to.  The two containers do not interact at
all---they only share the image---so the hacker space can be
started, restarted or reconfigured mid-event without touching the
game.  Since people arrive in drips and drabs, the account list is
whatever suits the room: a handful of shared sandboxes, or
one account per participant.

\subsection{Credentials}

The recommended path is the facilitator's Claude subscription: run
[[claude setup-token]] once on the facilitator's own machine and
pass the resulting token:

\begin{verbatim}
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-... make run
\end{verbatim}

Do \emph{not} try the seemingly simpler route of copying the
facilitator's logged-in credentials file,
[[~/.claude/.credentials.json]], into the team homes: those tokens
refresh-rotate, and three accounts refreshing the same token
concurrently will fail mid-event.  The long-lived setup token exists
precisely for this use case.

Two caveats come with the subscription route.  First, all teams
share one account's five-hour rolling rate limit; short turns on the
small default model should fit, but the limit is a single point of
throttling.  The instant fallback is to restart the container with
an API key instead ([[ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=... make run]])---a
budget-capped key doubles as the spending limit for the game.
Second, sharing a consumer account is the facilitator's conscious,
facilitator-operated choice to make.

\subsection{What persists where}

Everything worth keeping ends up under [[.data/]] on the host:

\begin{description}
\item[ [[.data/groups/<team>/]] ]  the team's project---their game,
  their documentation, their [[CLAUDE.md]];
\item[ [[.claude/projects/]] ]  inside each team directory: the
  session transcripts ([[*.jsonl]]), one file per connection, which
  the post-event evaluation reads;
\item[ [[.data/ssh/]] ]  the SSH host keys, so rebuilding the image
  never triggers \enquote{host identification changed} warnings.
\end{description}

The container itself holds no state: stop it, rebuild it, restart
it---as long as [[.data/]] survives, so does the event.  Turning that
directory into something a colleague can read and grade is the subject
of \cref{sec:harvest}.

\section{Harvesting the results}\label{sec:harvest}

When the event is over, [[.data/groups/]] holds everything worth
keeping---but in a form awkward to share.  Two obstacles stand in the
way of simply tarring it up on the host.  First, ownership: the entry
point handed each home to its team's in-container UID (2001, 2002,
\dots, \cref{sec:entrypoint}), which maps to no user on the
facilitator's laptop, and the transcripts are mode~600.  The host user
can traverse the directories but cannot read the files---and neither
can [[ccusage]] run as that user.  Second, the facilitator wants more
than the raw bytes: a readable summary of what each team built and how
to run it, and a tally of what each team's relay cost in tokens.

Only the \emph{first} obstacle truly needs the container.  Reading
mode-600 files owned by a foreign UID is a job for root, and the
throwaway container---root, with [[ccusage]] baked in
(\cref{sec:dockerfile})---is exactly the privileged reader we already
have.  Everything else is ordinary work: formatting numbers, asking an
LLM for a summary, rolling a tarball.  So we split the harvest in two.

\begin{description}
\item[Phase one, in the container]  As root, read the protected files:
  capture each team's usage and stage a pruned, \emph{host-owned} copy
  of the data.  Nothing that needs privilege is left for later.
\item[Phase two, on the host]  With the staged copy now readable, write
  the READMEs with the facilitator's \emph{own} [[claude]] and pack the
  archives with the host's own [[tar]] and [[zip]].
\end{description}

Running [[claude]] on the host rather than in the container is the
deliberate move.  The facilitator is already logged in---the same login
that paid for the event---so the harvest needs no credential plumbed
into a [[docker run]], and a host [[claude -p]] writes its transcript
under the facilitator's [[~/.claude]], never a team's, so it cannot
perturb the very usage we are reporting.  That usage is captured in
phase one, before phase two runs at all, which makes the separation
airtight: the figures are fixed on disk before the first summary is
written.

We cannot simply append the READMEs to a finished [[data.tar.gz]]---a
gzip stream is not appendable---so phase two writes them into the
staging tree first and packs once.  The one tree yields both
[[tar.gz]] and [[zip]], so the two archives are structurally identical.

The work is one script, [[team-report]], with three modes; the
[[Makefile]] wires them together:

\begin{description}
\item[ [[make ccusage]] ]  phase-one reading only: prints a per-team
  usage table.  No staging, no [[claude]];
\item[ [[make data.tar.gz]] / [[make data.zip]] ]  runs [[stage]] in
  the container, then [[pack]] on the host, producing both archives.
\end{description}

<<[[team-report]]>>=
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

<<harvest configuration>>
<<the usage formatter>>
<<collecting each team's usage>>
<<the usage report>>
<<staging the team data>>
<<packing the archive>>

<<dispatch on the requested mode>>
@

\subsection{Configuration}

A handful of constants.  [[groups]] is where the mounted volume exposes
the team homes, used by the two phase-one modes; the host phase takes
its staging directory as an argument instead, since on the host there
is no fixed mount point.  The formatter (below) is written to a fresh
temporary file the script can call in any mode.  The prune list names
the caches that are pure noise for an evaluator---npm's download cache,
Claude's own backups and shell snapshots, and the downloaded plugin
marketplace (tens of megabytes of example images, dwarfing the
transcripts)---so the archive carries the project and its transcripts,
not megabytes of recreatable cache.

<<harvest configuration>>=
groups=/data/groups
tmp="$(mktemp -d)"
fmt="$tmp/usagefmt.py"
prune=".npm .cache .local .claude/cache .claude/backups
       .claude/shell-snapshots .claude/plugins"
@

\subsection{Computing the usage}

The usage numbers come from [[ccusage]], which reads the [[.jsonl]]
transcripts under a config directory named by [[CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR]].
Pointing it at a team's [[.claude]] in turn is what makes the figures
\emph{per team} rather than for the machine as a whole; running inside
the container as root is what makes the mode-600 transcripts readable
at all.  [[collect_usage]] takes the directory to deposit the per-team
JSON in, because its two callers want it in different places: the
report mode in a throwaway temporary, the [[stage]] mode alongside the
data it is about to hand to the host.

<<collecting each team's usage>>=
collect_usage() {
  dest="$1"
  mkdir -p "$dest"
  for team in $TEAMS
  do
    CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$groups/$team/.claude" \
      ccusage session --json > "$dest/$team.json" 2>/dev/null \
      || printf '{"session":[],"totals":{}}\n' > "$dest/$team.json"
  done
}
@

A team that never ran a session has no transcripts, and [[ccusage]]
then exits non-zero; the fallback writes an empty-but-valid document so
the parser downstream need not special-case it.

The parsing lives in a small Python program rather than in the shell,
because the same three views of the data---a terminal table, a
per-team Markdown section, and a cross-team summary table---are far
easier to express in Python, and writing it once keeps the token and
cost arithmetic in a single place.  [[ccusage]] reports each session
under a [[session]] list (keyed by a [[period]] UUID, with its
[[modelsUsed]] and a [[metadata.lastActivity]] timestamp) and the
team aggregate under [[totals]]; the helpers below read exactly those
fields.

<<the usage formatter>>=
cat > "$fmt" <<'PYEOF'
<<usage-formatter Python>>
PYEOF
@

<<usage-formatter Python>>=
import json
import sys


def load(path):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {"session": [], "totals": {}}


def totals(doc):
    t = doc.get("totals") or {}
    return int(t.get("totalTokens") or 0), float(t.get("totalCost") or 0)


def fmt_int(n):
    return f"{n:,}"


def fmt_usd(c):
    return f"${c:,.2f}"


def session_rows(doc):
    for s in doc.get("session") or []:
        yield {
            "id": (s.get("period") or "")[:8],
            "when": ((s.get("metadata") or {}).get("lastActivity")
                     or "")[:10],
            "models": ", ".join(s.get("modelsUsed") or []),
            "tokens": int(s.get("totalTokens") or 0),
            "cost": float(s.get("totalCost") or 0),
        }
@

I split the heredoc here only to keep the narrative readable; noweb
concatenates the two [[usage-formatter Python]] chunks back into one
program.  The first holds the shared helpers, the next three each add a
command.  We dispatch on the first argument so the shell can ask for
whichever view it needs.

The terminal view, for [[make ccusage]], prints one block per team and
a grand total---enough for the facilitator to compare teams at a
glance.

<<usage-formatter Python>>=
def cmd_text(work, teams):
    grand_tokens = grand_cost = 0
    for team in teams:
        doc = load(f"{work}/{team}.json")
        tokens, cost = totals(doc)
        grand_tokens += tokens
        grand_cost += cost
        print(f"== {team} ==")
        rows = list(session_rows(doc))
        if not rows:
            print("  (no sessions)")
        for r in rows:
            print(f"  {r['id']}  {r['when']}  "
                  f"{fmt_int(r['tokens']):>14} tok  "
                  f"{fmt_usd(r['cost']):>10}  {r['models']}")
        print(f"  total: {fmt_int(tokens)} tok, {fmt_usd(cost)}")
        print()
    print(f"grand total: {fmt_int(grand_tokens)} tok, "
          f"{fmt_usd(grand_cost)}")
@

The per-team Markdown section is appended to each team's README.  We
build it from the captured numbers rather than asking [[claude]] to
transcribe them, so the figures in the archive are exact and not at the
mercy of a paraphrase.

<<usage-formatter Python>>=
def cmd_team_usage(path):
    doc = load(path)
    tokens, cost = totals(doc)
    print()
    print("## Token usage")
    print()
    print(f"- **Total tokens:** {fmt_int(tokens)}")
    print(f"- **Estimated cost:** {fmt_usd(cost)}")
    rows = list(session_rows(doc))
    if rows:
        print()
        print("| Session | Last activity | Models | Tokens | Cost |")
        print("|---|---|---|--:|--:|")
        for r in rows:
            print(f"| {r['id']} | {r['when']} | {r['models']} | "
                  f"{fmt_int(r['tokens'])} | {fmt_usd(r['cost'])} |")
@

The top-level summary table links to each team's own README and
borrows that README's first prose line as a one-line description of
what they built.  Reusing the line the model already wrote avoids a
second, separate [[claude]] call just for a sentence.

<<usage-formatter Python>>=
def first_prose_line(stage, team):
    try:
        with open(f"{stage}/{team}/README.md") as f:
            for line in f:
                line = line.strip()
                if line and line[0] not in "#|":
                    return line
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    return "—"


def cmd_summary(work, stage, teams):
    print("# TCS Summer Event — team contributions")
    print()
    print("Each team relay-built a small project in shared Claude "
          "sessions.  This archive carries every team's work and the "
          "transcripts that produced it; follow a team's link for what "
          "they built, how to run it, and their token usage.")
    print()
    print("| Team | What they built | Tokens | Cost |")
    print("|---|---|--:|--:|")
    grand_tokens = grand_cost = 0
    for team in teams:
        tokens, cost = totals(load(f"{work}/{team}.json"))
        grand_tokens += tokens
        grand_cost += cost
        desc = first_prose_line(stage, team)
        print(f"| [{team}]({team}/README.html) | {desc} | "
              f"{fmt_int(tokens)} | {fmt_usd(cost)} |")
    print(f"| **Grand total** | | **{fmt_int(grand_tokens)}** | "
          f"**{fmt_usd(grand_cost)}** |")
@

<<usage-formatter Python>>=
command = sys.argv[1]
if command == "text":
    cmd_text(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3:])
elif command == "team-usage":
    cmd_team_usage(sys.argv[2])
elif command == "summary":
    cmd_summary(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4:])
@

\subsection{The usage report}

In [[ccusage]] mode the script does nothing but collect and print: no
staging, no [[claude]].  That is the whole point of having a mode---the
facilitator can check the budget at any time, even mid-event from a
second terminal, without touching the archive.  It collects into a
throwaway temporary, since these numbers are wanted on the terminal,
not on disk.

<<the usage report>>=
print_report() {
  collect_usage "$tmp"
  python3 "$fmt" text "$tmp" $TEAMS
}
@

\subsection{Phase one: staging, in the container}

[[stage]] is the only privileged step.  It captures the usage beside
the data (so phase two need not---and cannot---recompute it), copies
each team's home wholesale and deletes the prune list, then hands the
whole tree to the host with a single [[chown -R]].  Deleting the prune
list rather than naming what to keep errs on the side of keeping a
team's actual work.  Usage lands in [[$out/usage]] and the data in
[[$out/data]], kept apart so the usage sidecar never ends up inside the
archive.  [[HOST_UID]] and [[HOST_GID]] are passed in because the
container cannot otherwise know who ran [[make]].

<<staging the team data>>=
stage_data() {
  out="$1"
  collect_usage "$out/usage"
  mkdir -p "$out/data"
  for team in $TEAMS
  do
    cp -a "$groups/$team" "$out/data/$team"
    for cache in $prune
    do
      rm -rf "$out/data/$team/$cache"
    done
  done
  chown -R "$HOST_UID:$HOST_GID" "$out"
}
@

\subsection{Phase two: packing, on the host}

[[pack]] runs on the host, on the now-readable staging tree, and uses
the facilitator's own tools throughout.  It writes each team's README,
assembles the top-level one, renders the lot to HTML, and rolls both
archives into the directory [[make]] was invoked from.  Because
everything it touches is already host-owned, it needs no privilege and
no [[chown]].

The READMEs are written as Markdown but shipped as HTML: an evaluator
opening the archive double-clicks [[README.html]] in a browser and the
team links in the summary table are live, clicking straight through to
each team's own [[README.html]].  The conversion is deliberately the
\emph{last} step before packing---the summary table borrows each team's
first prose line from its Markdown (\cref{sec:harvest}), so the
[[.md]] files must still exist when the summary is assembled.  Only
once the summary is on disk do we render everything and drop the
Markdown.

<<packing the archive>>=
pack_archive() {
  out="$1"
  start="$(pwd)"
  data="$out/data"
  usage="$out/usage"
  <<write each team's README>>
  python3 "$fmt" summary "$usage" "$data" $TEAMS > "$data/README.md"
  <<render the READMEs to HTML>>
  <<pack both archives afresh>>
}
@

We delete any earlier archives before packing.  [[tar -czf]] truncates
its output, but [[zip -r]] \emph{updates} an existing archive in
place---it adds and refreshes entries but never drops ones whose files
have since disappeared.  Without the [[rm]], a second run (or a stale
archive left in the working directory) would keep yesterday's now-gone
[[README.md]] entries alongside today's [[README.html]], so the two
archives would disagree about their own contents.  Removing first makes
the pack idempotent and the [[.tar.gz]] and [[.zip]] structurally
identical, as \cref{sec:harvest} promised.

<<pack both archives afresh>>=
rm -f "$start/data.tar.gz" "$start/data.zip"
( cd "$data" && tar -czf "$start/data.tar.gz" . )
( cd "$data" && zip -qr "$start/data.zip" . )
@

Each team's README is the one place [[claude]] is used, and we
assemble it from three deterministic parts: a heading we print, the
prose [[claude]] writes, and the usage section appended from the JSON
captured in phase one (not regenerated, so the figures in the archive
are exactly those phase one settled).  Keeping the heading and the
table in the shell---rather than asking [[claude]] for them---means the
model is responsible only for the description and run instructions, in
British English to match the rest of the event's prose.

The subtle part is \emph{how} we take [[claude]]'s prose.  Capturing
its stdout, as an earlier version did, bakes far more than the document
into the archive: this harvest runs the facilitator's own
[[claude]], whose [[~/.claude]] carries a time-tracking hook that
announces itself (\enquote{Started tracking\dots}) and an explanatory
output style that appends an \enquote{Insight} block to every reply---all
on stdout, ahead of and after the actual answer.  So instead we have
[[claude]] \emph{write} its prose to a file with the Write tool and
read that file back, discarding stdout entirely
(\cref{sec:claude-body}).  This also repairs the summary table:
[[first_prose_line]] (\cref{sec:harvest}) now meets the team's real
first sentence rather than the hook's status line.

<<write each team's README>>=
for team in $TEAMS
do
  files="$(<<the team's contribution files>>)"
  readme="$data/$team/README.md"
  printf '# %s\n\n' "$team" > "$readme"
  if [ -n "$files" ]
  then
    <<summarise the team's work with Claude>>
  else
    printf '_No project files were produced._\n' >> "$readme"
  fi
  python3 "$fmt" team-usage "$usage/$team.json" >> "$readme"
done
@

The contribution files are the team-authored ones at the top of the
home: we skip the seeds [[CLAUDE.md]] and [[RULES.md]] (identical for
every team, so no evidence of what \emph{this} team did) and every
dotfile (configuration and the [[.claude]] tree, not project work).

<<the team's contribution files>>=
find "$data/$team" -maxdepth 1 -type f \
  ! -name CLAUDE.md ! -name RULES.md ! -name '.*' | sort
@

\subsection{Taking Claude's prose through a file}
\label{sec:claude-body}

The prompt inlines each file under a header naming it, then asks for a
first line of plain description (which the summary table later reuses,
\cref{sec:harvest}) followed by run instructions.  We also ask it to
render any file the reader should open as a Markdown link to that file
by its bare name: the README is written beside the team's own files,
so a relative link such as [[[game.html](game.html)]] resolves to the
sibling once \cref{sec:render-html} turns it into an anchor---the
evaluator launches the game from the README with one click rather than
hunting for the file in the tree.  The one change from
a plain [[claude -p]] is the destination: we instruct it to put the
documentation in a file named [[body.md]] with the Write tool, and we
run it inside a fresh, empty scratch directory whose only purpose is to
receive that file.  Confining the single writable tool to a throwaway
directory keeps the safety property the inline-files design already
had---[[claude]] is given no way to read the team's data and now no way
to modify it either, so it still cannot wander.  We read [[body.md]]
back and append it; if it is missing or empty (the model failed, or the
Write was refused), a placeholder keeps the README well-formed rather
than aborting the whole archive.

<<summarise the team's work with Claude>>=
scratch="$(mktemp -d)"
{
  printf '%s\n' \
    "You are documenting a team's contribution for an" \
    "evaluation archive.  Write in British English." \
    "Use the Write tool to create a file named body.md in the" \
    "current directory containing exactly the documentation and" \
    "nothing else: begin with one plain sentence, on its own" \
    "line and with no heading, saying what the team built; then" \
    "a section headed \"### How to run it\" with exact," \
    "step-by-step instructions to run or open the project." \
    "Whenever you name a file the reader should open or run," \
    "write it as a Markdown link to that file by its bare" \
    "name, for example [game.html](game.html), so they can" \
    "launch it with one click; the documentation sits in the" \
    "same directory as these files." \
    "Describe only what the files below show; invent nothing." \
    "The files:"
  for file in $files
  do
    printf '\n--- %s ---\n' "$(basename "$file")"
    cat "$file"
  done
} | ( cd "$scratch" && claude -p --allowedTools Write \
        --permission-mode acceptEdits >/dev/null 2>&1 )
if [ -s "$scratch/body.md" ]
then
  cat "$scratch/body.md" >> "$readme"
else
  printf '_Summary unavailable._\n' >> "$readme"
fi
rm -rf "$scratch"
@

\subsection{Rendering the READMEs to HTML}
\label{sec:render-html}

We render with [[pandoc]] in \emph{standalone} mode ([[-s]]): a bare
HTML fragment has no [[<head>]] and many browsers then mis-guess the
encoding, whereas a standalone document opens cleanly on a
double-click---the whole point of shipping HTML rather than Markdown.
[[pandoc]] is a host tool, in keeping with phase two using the
facilitator's own tools throughout.

Standalone mode wants a document title for the [[<title>]] element; we
lift it from the file's first [[#]] heading, which both generators
above already emit as line one (the team name, or the event title for
the summary).  Reusing that heading means the browser tab is labelled
without inventing a second source of truth.  We delete each Markdown
file once rendered so the archive carries only the browser-openable
form, as requested.

<<render the READMEs to HTML>>=
for md in "$data/README.md" "$data"/*/README.md
do
  title="$(head -n1 "$md" | sed 's/^#* *//')"
  pandoc -s --metadata title="$title" -o "${md%.md}.html" "$md"
  rm "$md"
done
@

\subsection{Dispatch}

The three modes map to the two phases: [[ccusage]] and [[stage]] are
the in-container readers, [[pack]] is the host finisher.  Each takes
its own output location, so nothing is assumed about where the script
runs.

<<dispatch on the requested mode>>=
case "${1:-ccusage}" in
  ccusage) print_report ;;
  stage)   stage_data "$2" ;;
  pack)    pack_archive "$2" ;;
  *)
    echo "usage: team-report {ccusage|stage <dir>|pack <dir>}" >&2
    exit 1
    ;;
esac
@

\end{document}
